  "live_records": {
    "q-163111": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163111",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "For the function of two variables \n$f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-y+1),$\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=ax-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+bxy+c{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$",
      "worked_solution": "If the function $f(x,y)$ is twice differentiable at $(x,y)=(0,0),$ then the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $(0,0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈\\overset{\\text{Linear approximation}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0,0)+{f}_{x}(0,0)\\cdot x+{f}_{y}(0,0)\\cdot y}} \\\\  & \\,\\,+{{\\frac{1}{2}({f}_{xx}(0,0)\\cdot {x}^{2}+2{f}_{xy}(0,0)\\cdot xy+{f}_{yy}(0,0)\\cdot {y}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{\\text{Quadratic part}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the terms of our Taylor polynomial.\n\n- Calculating the value of the function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the first derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{x-y+1}, & \\,{f}_{y}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{x-y+1} \\\\ {f}_{x}(0,0) & =1, & \\,{f}_{y}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Calculating the second derivatives, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xx}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xx}(0,0) & =-1 \\\\ {f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{xy}(0,0) & =1 \\\\ {f}_{yy}(x,y) & =-\\frac{1}{(x-y+1)^{2}}, & \\,{f}_{yy}(0,0) & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & ≈0+1\\cdot x+(-1)\\cdot y+\\frac{1}{2}(-1\\cdot {x}^{2}+2(1)\\cdot xy+(-1)\\cdot {y}^{2}) \\\\  & =1x-y-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+1xy+(-\\frac{1}{2}){y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\na+b+c=1+1+(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
