    "q-163102": {
      "dom_id": "step-q163102",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "For the function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+xy,\n$$\n\nthe quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & ∗ \\\\ 2 & ∗\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}∗ \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFind the sum of the three missing expressions.",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+y & \\frac{x}{y}+x\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nCalculating the second derivative, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & \\frac{1}{y}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}+1 & -\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {f}^{″}(1,1) & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y-1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, the sum of the missing expressions is\n\n$$\n2+(-1)+(x-1)=x.\n$$",
