    "q-164828": {
      "dom_id": "step-q164828",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "For the twice-differentiable function $f:{R}^{2}\\to R,$ we have the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,-1) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(1,-1) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUse the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ to approximate the value of $f(0,0).$",
      "worked_solution": "Suppose that the function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R$ is defined by \n\n$$\nf:[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]⟶z\n$$\n\n \n\nand is twice differentiable at $x=a.$ Then, the quadratic Taylor polynomial of $f$ about $a$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ≈f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{1}{2}(x-a)^{T}{f}^{″}(a)(x-a),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]}_{x=a}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we have $a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ and the following data:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(a) & =-e, & \\,{f}^{′}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}], & \\,{f}^{″}(a) & =[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second-degree Taylor polynomial is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)≈-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x-1 \\\\ y+1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the polynomial at $x=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,0) & ≈-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0-1 \\\\ 0+1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-e+[\\begin{aligned}-e & e\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{1}{2}{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}^{T}[\\begin{aligned}-e & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =-e+2e+\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}e \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =e+\\frac{1}{2}(-e+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{e+1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
